 Weight Measurement\n\nThomas M. Schmitt\n\nUltraviolet/Visible Spectroscopy\n\nGeorge D. Mills\n\nX-Ray Analysis\n\nA. Monroe Snider\n\nPaint and Coating Specifications and Standards\n\nJoseph V. Koleske\n\nRadiation Curing of Coatings\n\nJoseph V. Koleske\n\nPowder Coating\n\nJoseph V. Koleske\n\nAppendix Free\n\nFormat\n\n[Select]\n\nPrice:\n\nAdd to Cart\n\nShipping & Handling\n\n###### Details\n\nDeveloped by Committee: D01\n\nPages: 1000\n\nDOI: 10.1520/MNL17-2ND-EB\n\nISBN-EB: 978-0-8031-8891-4\n\nISBN-13: 978-0-8031-7017-9--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTesting Paint and Coatings | ASTM (https://www.astm.org/news/testing-paint-and-coatings-jf12)\nciteturn29search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.1 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Last published in 1995, and originally published as the Gardner-Sward Handbook, the new 15th edition provides a review of past, present and foreseeable testing trends within the scope of the sponsoring group, ASTM Committee D01 on Paint and Related Coatings, Materials and Applications. ... As noted in the manual introduction, \"Today the [powder coating] technology is well established, has a significant share of the coatings market, has an international acceptance and has a strong technical society that aids in future growth.\"\n\nImage: News image\n\nBy Cicely Enright\n\nJul 26, 2016\n\n## A Newly Revamped and Expanded Industry Manual from ASTM\n\nThe paint and coatings industry continues to innovate with new technology, and accompanying that is a newly updated manual for use by the industry. The work is Manual 17, Paint and Coating Testing Manual, and ASTM International has now published the 15th edition of this time-honored and respected book.\n\nThe insights in this updated and expanded edition give technologists and managers, novices and experts a useful reference about the many facets of the industry. Last published in 1995, and originally published as the Gardner-Sward Handbook, the new 15th edition provides a review of past, present and foreseeable testing trends within the scope of the sponsoring group, ASTM Committee D01 on Paint and Related Coatings, Materials and Applications.\n\nTwo chapters have been added about powder coating and radiation curing of coatings to reflect advances in these areas. As noted in the manual introduction, \"Today the [powder coating] technology is well established, has a significant share of the coatings market, has an international acceptance and has a strong technical society that aids in future growth.\" --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEffects of nano-TiO2 particles addition on fluidization of flaky fluorophlogopite powder for the CVD preparation of pearlescent pigments - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0032591023001985)\nciteturn29search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; The addition of nano-TiO_{2} particles leads to competitive deposition between free TiO_{2} particles and fluorophlogopite particles, which decreased the coating rate of TiO_{2} on fluorophlogopite particle. ... The gas-solid ratio primarily determines the number of the free TiO_{2} particles on pearlescent pigment particles. ... The key problems of preparing polysilicon in FBCVD is the chemical mechanism and the generation of fine powder [10,11].\n\n## Powder Technology\n\nDate: 1 May 2023\n\nArticle: 118414\n\nVolume: Volume 421\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Effects of nano-TiO_{2} particles addition on fluidization of flaky fluorophlogopite powder for the CVD preparation of pearlescent pigments\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Ming Liu ^{a}, Guoqiang Shao ^{a}, Pengpeng Lv ^{a}, Jun Li ^{a}, Qingshan Zhu ^{a} ^{b}\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.powtec.2023.118414\n\n\n## Highlights\n\n  * •\n\nFlaky fluorophlogopite particles were fluidized by adding nano-TiO_{2} particles.\n\n  * •\n\nExcess addition of nano-TiO_{2} particles lead large aggregates affecting performance of pearlescent pigment.\n\n  * •\n\nThe optimal value of addition ratio of nano-TiO_{2} in fluorophlogopite powder is 0.4 wt%.\n\n  * •\n\nA decreasing gas-solid of TiCl_{4} vapor and fluorophlogopite powder improved the glossiness.\n\n  * •\n\nThe gas-solid ratio of TiCl_{4} vapor and fluorophlogopite powder is ∼0.05 m^{3}kg^{−1} h^{−1}.\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe fluidization quality significantly affects the mass transfer and the deposition reaction, which determine the uniformity of deposited TiO_{2} thin films on fluorophlogopite particles. It was found that the effective addition ratio of nano-TiO_{2} particles is above 0.2 wt% to realize fluidization of fluorophlogopite particles. The addition of nano-TiO_{2} particles leads to competitive deposition between free TiO_{2} particles and fluorophlogopite particles, which decreased the coating rate of TiO_{2} on fluorophlogopite particle. Besides, the existence of free TiO_{2} particles on pearlescent pigment particles decreases the glossiness. The gas-solid ratio primarily determines the number of the free TiO_{2} particles on pearlescent pigment particles. It was summarized that the optimal value of addition ratio of nano-TiO_{2} is 0.4 wt% with the gas-solid ratio of ∼0.05 m^{3}kg^{−1} h^{−1} to enhance the fluidization quality on the one hand, and to reduce the free TiO_{2} particles on the other hand.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDetermination of the critical pigment volume concentrations of pigmented film coating formulations using gloss measurement - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0378517388902943)\nciteturn29search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; # Determination of the critical pigment volume concentrations of pigmented film coating formulations using gloss measurement ... The values vary between 7 and 12% for the iron oxide pigments, 13.5 and 15% for titanium dioxide and 12 and 35% for talcs of varying particle size.\n\n## International Journal of Pharmaceutics\n\nDate: August 1988\n\nPages: 245-248\n\nVolume: Volume 45, Issue 3\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Determination of the critical pigment volume concentrations of pigmented film coating formulations using gloss measurement\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel S.H.M. Gibson ^{1}, R.C. Rowe ^{2}, E.F.T. White ^{1}\n\n\n10.1016/0378-5173(88)90294-3\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDFPatient Access\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article preview\n  * Cited by (12)\n\n## Abstract\n\nCritical pigment volume concentrations for a variety of pigments and opacifiers dispersed in a hydroxypropyl methylcellulose film have been determined using gloss measurement. The values vary between 7 and 12% for the iron oxide pigments, 13.5 and 15% for titanium dioxide and 12 and 35% for talcs of varying particle size. The results are comparable with those determined using other methods and are consistent with the known theories of pigment dispersion and packing.\n\n## Keywords\n\nCritical pigment volume concentration\n\n;\n\nFilm coating\n\n;\n\nGloss measurement\n\n\n## References (10)\n\n  * W.K. Asbeck et al.\n\n### Critical pigment volume relationships\n\n### Ind. Eng. Chem.\n\n(1949)\n\n  * J.G. Brodnyan\n\n### Adsorption of hydroxyethyl cellulose onto titanium dioxide\n\n### J. Paint Technol.\n\n(1968)\n\n  * G. Guillaume\n\n### CPVC determination with a goniophotometer\n\n### Paint Technol.\n\n(1969)\n\n  * A.O. Okamafe et al.\n\n### Effect of solids-polymer interactions on the properties of some aqueous-based tablet film coating formulations. I. Moisture permeability\n\n### Int. J. Pharm.\n\n(1984)\n\n  * A.O. Okamafe et al.\n\n### Effect of solids-polymer interactions on the properties of some aqueous-based tablet film coating formulations. II. Mechanical characteristics\n\n### Int. J. Pharm.\n\n(1984)\n\nThere are more references available in the full text version of this article.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe pigmentation of powder coatings with the use of block copolymer dispersants - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0300944099000545)\nciteturn29search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; The incomplete dispersion under standard extrusion conditions can be attributed to a combination of poor pigment wetting and insufficient disruption of the pigment agglomerates, as a result of the rheological properties of the powder coating resin. ... Indirect methods, such as the use of the flocculation gradient technique or the determination of gloss and opacity, are employed successfully [1], [3], [27], [28], but in most cases these properties are influenced by other factors as well.\n\n## Progress in Organic Coatings\n\nVolume 36, Issue 4, September 1999, Pages 225-230\n\n# The pigmentation of powder coatings with the use of block copolymer dispersants\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/S0300-9440(99)00054-5Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe dispersing of pigments in powder coatings is a difficult and complicated process. The incomplete dispersion under standard extrusion conditions can be attributed to a combination of poor pigment wetting and insufficient disruption of the pigment agglomerates, as a result of the rheological properties of the powder coating resin. The use of block copolymer dispersants for improving the pigment dispersion in powder coatings is studied in this project. Novel block copolymer dispersants from 2-vinylpyridine and ϵ-caprolactone have been synthesized and tested. These block copolymers show a high affinity for TiO_{2} pigments and are capable of improving the stability of a TiO_{2} dispersion in a polyester powder coating resin.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOptical analyses of pigment particles in colour concentrates and polypropylene yarns - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S014294180100143X)\nciteturn29search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Titanium dioxide is well dispersed in one analysed monobatch (WI601PCO) but the other contains an amount of small aggregates not to be ignored. ... Coloured polypropylene fibres are obtained mostly by the spin-dyeing technique in which pigments are added to the polymer melt during extrusion.Besides their colouring effect on fibres, pigments also have an influence on the transparency and the gloss of the end-product.In order to obtain an even colour distribution, it is essential for the pigment to be well dispersed in the medium.\nTitanium dioxide is well dispersed in one analysed monobatch (WI601PCO) but the other contains an amount of small aggregates not to be ignored. Carbon black is generally well dispersed.\n\n## Keywords\n\nPolypropylene\n\n;\n\nPigments\n\n;\n\nColour concentrates\n\n;\n\nYarns\n\n;\n\nOptical microscopy\n\n## Introduction\n\nColoured polypropylene fibres are obtained mostly by the spin-dyeing technique in which pigments are added to the polymer melt during extrusion. Besides their colouring effect on fibres, pigments also have an influence on the transparency and the gloss of the end-product. In order to obtain an even colour distribution, it is essential for the pigment to be well dispersed in the medium. The primary size of the particles has to be approached as closely as possible and the number of pigment aggregates and agglomerates has to be reduced to a minimum [6].\n\nIn Fig. 1 some examples of primary particles, aggregates and agglomerates are illustrated. Primary particles can assume a variety of shapes, such as cubes, platelets, needles or bars, as well as a number of irregular shapes. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStudy on application performance of oxidized polyethylene wax in powder coatings - ScienceDirect (https://www.s